Global Exception Handling
Purpose
Handles exceptions centrally for the entire application.
Avoids writing try-catch blocks in every controller.
Returns meaningful HTTP responses instead of generic 500 Internal Server Error.
Components
1. Custom Exception

Example:

public class UserNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {

    public UserNotFoundException(String message){
        super(message);
    }
}
Purpose

Represents a specific business error.

Instead of

throw new RuntimeException("...");

use

throw new UserNotFoundException("...");

This makes the code more meaningful and readable.

2. super(message)
Purpose

Calls the constructor of RuntimeException and stores the error message.

Example

super("User not found");

Later,

ex.getMessage();

returns

User not found
3. @ControllerAdvice
Purpose

Marks the class as a Global Exception Handler.

Whenever an exception is thrown from any controller, Spring checks this class for a matching @ExceptionHandler.

Without it:

Exception
   ↓
500 Internal Server Error

With it:

Exception
   ↓
@ControllerAdvice
   ↓
Proper Response
4. @ExceptionHandler

Example

@ExceptionHandler(UserNotFoundException.class)
Purpose

Tells Spring:

If UserNotFoundException occurs, call this method.

Flow
Client
   ↓
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
throw UserNotFoundException
   ↓
Spring Framework
   ↓
@ControllerAdvice
   ↓
@ExceptionHandler(UserNotFoundException.class)
   ↓
404 NOT FOUND

Interview Questions
Q1. What is Global Exception Handling?

Global Exception Handling provides a centralized way to handle exceptions across the entire application instead of using try-catch blocks in every controller.

Q2. Why use @ControllerAdvice?

It marks the class as a global exception handler so Spring can route exceptions from any controller to it.

Q3. What does @ExceptionHandler(UserNotFoundException.class) do?

It tells Spring to invoke this method whenever a UserNotFoundException is thrown.

Q4. Why create a custom exception?

To represent business-specific errors clearly and make exception handling more meaningful.

Q5. Why super(message)?

It stores the error message in the parent RuntimeException, allowing us to retrieve it later using getMessage().

Q6. Does the Service call GlobalExceptionHandler?

No. The Service simply throws the exception. Spring automatically catches it and invokes the matching @ExceptionHandler method.

Q7. Can one GlobalExceptionHandler handle multiple exceptions?

Yes. One @ControllerAdvice class can contain multiple @ExceptionHandler methods, each handling a different exception.

Example:

@ExceptionHandler(UserNotFoundException.class)

@ExceptionHandler(EmailAlreadyExistsException.class)

@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)

@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
Key Points
UserNotFoundException → Defines what went wrong.
@ControllerAdvice → Registers a global exception handler.
@ExceptionHandler → Maps a specific exception to a handler method.
ResponseEntity → Returns the appropriate HTTP status and response body.
